  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a continuum from real clinical interaction to augmented reality (AR) and fully virtual reality (VR), and shows how these tools may be used in healthcare and body perception tasks. At the top, the same patient–clinician scene is presented in three modes: direct face-to-face contact in a real room, an AR version where digital elements are layered onto the real setting through glasses, and a VR version where the user wears a headset and experiences an entirely simulated encounter.\n\nThe lower panels give concrete examples. One shows a shoulder injection while a haptic device is attached near the treatment area, suggesting touch or sensory feedback during a medical procedure. Another depicts a person viewing themselves in a mirror while wearing AR glasses, with a highlighted “virtual limb” replacing or supplementing a “missing limb,” indicating body ownership or rehabilitation applications. The final example contrasts a patient lying in a hospital bed wearing a VR headset with a calmer virtual environment, implying immersive distraction or therapeutic relocation from the clinical room.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that immersive technologies can range from overlaying virtual body parts onto reality to replacing the surrounding environment entirely for treatment, training, or symptom management.",
